    mechanics: "Ring all-reduce of S bytes over n ranks sends and receives about `2 × S × (n − 1) / n` bytes per rank in `2 × (n − 1)` steps: bandwidth-optimal but latency grows with n. Tree algorithms cut latency to `O(log n)` steps and are preferred for small messages or many ranks; NCCL selects among ring, tree and hardware-offloaded reductions automatically. All-reduce is exactly reduce-scatter followed by all-gather, which is why ZeRO / FSDP can split it around the optimizer step at no extra cost. Time for one collective is roughly `latency + bytes / effective_bandwidth`, where effective bandwidth is the slowest link in the group: intra-node NVLink / NVSwitch provides hundreds of GB/s per GPU, inter-node network ports commonly tens of GB/s, so groups that span nodes run at the inter-node rate. Collectives are issued on streams and overlap with computation when the data they need is ready early (gradients by bucket, parameters by layer prefetch). Process groups define which ranks participate: a 3D layout has separate DP, TP and PP groups, plus EP and CP groups when used, each with its own collectives. A collective completes only when its slowest rank arrives, so load imbalance anywhere becomes exposed communication everywhere.",
